his own&nbsp; protection.Now he looks like Dave Letterman with&nbsp;&nbsp; a post-beard—like a [ __ ] wild man living—he&nbsp; looks like a crazy person. He didn't used to&nbsp;&nbsp; look like that at all. He used to look like a&nbsp; Williamsburg man.Okay, Nicholas Franco asks:&nbsp;&nbsp; Why do we think the majority of Jews coming from&nbsp; the Soviet Union are so right-wing?I don't know&nbsp;&nbsp; if that's necessarily true. But I mean, you know,&nbsp; look—the thing is, my take is always that refugees&nbsp;&nbsp; tend to be the ones who leave their home country&nbsp; because they're opposed to the ideology of their&nbsp;&nbsp; home country, which is like the anti-Castro&nbsp; Cubans and so on. So the Soviet Union was a&nbsp;&nbsp; socialist country. If you didn't love socialism,&nbsp; you left, right? What do you think?Yeah, I think&nbsp;&nbsp; that's right. Although it is my understanding&nbsp; that Ukrainian Jews who have left Ukraine to&nbsp;&nbsp; move to Israel are exceedingly right-wing. I've&nbsp; even spoken to Israeli journalists who have told&nbsp;&nbsp; me that there are so many right-wing Ukrainian&nbsp; Jews that have moved there just over the last&nbsp;&nbsp; twenty or twenty-five years that it's pushed the&nbsp; entire electorate to the right.Crazy. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp; that is a country that has a lot of immigration,&nbsp; and their demographics change really radically in&nbsp;&nbsp; a relatively short amount of time as a result.&nbsp; We don't have that level of immigration in this&nbsp;&nbsp; country. Our demographics shift slowly.Yeah, we&nbsp; do. We shift slowly. And a country of only nine&nbsp;&nbsp; million people can shift very quickly when&nbsp; you have these huge influxes.Yeah, a hundred&nbsp;&nbsp; thousand is a lot of people in a country of nine&nbsp; million people.That's right.Quadruple J XYZ:&nbsp;&nbsp; Do we think that the unveiled corruption in&nbsp; Ukraine—unveiled my ass—it's like it's always&nbsp;&nbsp; been in plain sight—will influence Western&nbsp; support in any ways?It's a great question,&nbsp;&nbsp; though. We're talking about, of course, the&nbsp; recent scandal of the people surrounding Zelensky&nbsp;&nbsp; in this energy-bribery kind of thing, which kind&nbsp; of has echoes of the Hunter Biden-Burisma [ __ ].&nbsp;&nbsp; What do you think, John?I don't think that the&nbsp; US is going to back away from Ukraine. Corruption&nbsp;&nbsp; goes with the territory over there. And it's&nbsp; not unusual. Doesn't it remind you a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp; of Hamid Karzai, where basically everyone around&nbsp; you is corrupt as [ __ ], but no one can prove that&nbsp;&nbsp; you personally took money? This isn't like Eric&nbsp; Adams, who is personally corrupt and had credible&nbsp;&nbsp; charges filed against him. Nobody really—Zelensky&nbsp; suddenly is rich and has this place on Lake Como,&nbsp;&nbsp; but maybe he just has a lot of good residuals from&nbsp; his acting career. We can't prove it, right? But&nbsp;&nbsp; like Hamid Karzai, you certainly could never prove&nbsp; that he personally was lining his pockets. This is&nbsp;&nbsp; kind of like President Harding or something like&nbsp; that.I think that's a good way to say it. And&nbsp;&nbsp; President Harding is probably the right comparison&nbsp; to make.Yeah, I agree with that. But you know&nbsp;&nbsp; what's painful too, Ted, is that nobody seems&nbsp; to really care when leaders are corrupt now.No.&nbsp;&nbsp; Nobody cares whether we're talking about domestic&nbsp; or foreign affairs. Nobody cares when their own&nbsp;&nbsp; side is called out. Basically there's never&nbsp; a thing where it's like, oh, I supported this&nbsp;&nbsp; candidate or this party, but now I've learned new&nbsp; information that they're a turd, so therefore I no&nbsp;&nbsp; longer support them anymore. That doesn't happen&nbsp; anymore. You just make excuses or you brush it&nbsp;&nbsp; off or you do whataboutism, but you don't change&nbsp; your mind. You 