t so out of control? What caused it&nbsp; to happen before '75? I know the Church Committee&nbsp;&nbsp; came in afterwards and there were all the scandals&nbsp; because of Philip Agee and all that. But when the&nbsp;&nbsp; CIA is more under control, what's the difference&nbsp; between that and a time like now when they're&nbsp;&nbsp; out of control? Is it leadership? What is it?It is&nbsp; the nature of the CIA. It's the collective mindset&nbsp;&nbsp; of the CIA to push the envelope as far as you can&nbsp; push until somebody in the White House pushes back&nbsp;&nbsp; or somebody on Capitol Hill pushes back. It's the&nbsp; culture. They called it "kingdom building" when&nbsp;&nbsp; I was there. Everybody in a position of authority&nbsp; at the CIA wants to build his or her own kingdom.&nbsp;&nbsp; And so you just keep pushing the boundaries of&nbsp; legality until somebody pushes back. And the next&nbsp;&nbsp; thing you know, they're doing anything they damn&nbsp; well please. There's almost zero congressional&nbsp;&nbsp; oversight. And at the White House, they just&nbsp; throw up their hands and say, well, what can we&nbsp;&nbsp; do? There's nothing we can do.And these days it's&nbsp; the lack of interest in reining them in coming&nbsp;&nbsp; from the White House, you would say?I would. And&nbsp; I would say the inability to rein them in on the&nbsp;&nbsp; part of the congressional oversight committees.Why&nbsp; are they unable?Because there are about forty&nbsp;&nbsp; employees of the Senate Intelligence Committee and&nbsp; about sixty employees of the House Intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp; Committee and forty thousand employees at the&nbsp; CIA.That's a good answer. You just can't handle&nbsp;&nbsp; it.Okay. Peru's a JFK-related question: You met&nbsp; or worked with David Talbot? And what do you think&nbsp;&nbsp; about his work? My impression is that he's not&nbsp; in good health nowadays. I'm sorry to hear that,&nbsp;&nbsp; David. Poor David.Yeah. David wrote the foreword&nbsp; for my third book. He's a lovely guy and one of&nbsp;&nbsp; the most highly respected contemporary historians&nbsp; that's out there. He had a debilitating serious&nbsp;&nbsp; stroke—his second stroke—a few months ago. And the&nbsp; last I heard he was unable to care for himself,&nbsp;&nbsp; unable to speak, and certainly unable to write.&nbsp; The poor guy.He was the editor of Salon.Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp; he was. So my only interaction was trying to get&nbsp; him to run my cartoons. He ran the cartoons of&nbsp;&nbsp; four of my colleagues who were very similar to the&nbsp; kind of work that I did, so I thought I kind of&nbsp;&nbsp; belonged naturally in that orbit. He was nice,&nbsp; but obviously he didn't like my work and just&nbsp;&nbsp; didn't want to say so. And he never ran it ever.&nbsp; And it was always a source of frustration to me.&nbsp;&nbsp; But he's allowed to not like my work. That was my&nbsp; interaction with him. He was always polite, albeit&nbsp;&nbsp; a little weaselly about it. Editors.USXXXArod21,&nbsp; thanks for the $2. Have you seen the story about&nbsp;&nbsp; the FSB attacking Polish train tracks with C4&nbsp; explosives? Was Poland right to worry about Putin&nbsp;&nbsp; and his ambitions? Or is this mind games Putin is&nbsp; playing? Is there a real issue? In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp; is there a real danger of Russia invading Poland&nbsp; here?Yeah. Yes, the Poles have reason to be&nbsp;&nbsp; worried.Really?Yeah, I do. I do believe that.&nbsp; Listen, the Russians are just like every other&nbsp;&nbsp; country, just like every other superpower or&nbsp; regional superpower. They're going to carry out&nbsp;&nbsp; operations against the countries that they&nbsp; believe are threats to them. And sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp; those operations are large-scale and sometimes&nbsp; they're small-scale, but sabotage is just a&nbsp;&nbsp; normal thing. It's almost an act of war, right?&nbsp; Going into a sovereign state and—Transportation&nbsp;&nbsp; infrastructure. Absolutely.But listen, even if&nbsp; they got caught, nobody's going to go to war over&nbsp;&nbsp; some train tracks.I got it. But I just—I don't&nbsp; think that Russia would invade Poland.No, no,&nbsp;&nbsp; I don't think absolutely not, because to invade&nbsp; Poland is to invade NATO, and then it's going to&nbsp;&nbsp; require a massive response.Destined for Nothing&nbsp; says: John, I saw a job posting yesterday—now&nbsp;&nbsp; removed—for a Director of Identity Intelligence&nbsp; at Health and Human Services—HHS. Can you shed&nbsp;&nbsp; some light on what that could mean?A Director&nbsp; of Identity Intelligence. The word "identity"&nbsp;&nbsp; throws me. I honestly don't know what that could&nbsp; mean. It's not unusual for almost every federal&nbsp;&nbsp; department to have an intelligence office. For&nbsp; example, there's a CIA representative from the&nbsp;&nbsp; analytics side—from the Directorate of&nbsp; Intelligence—at Treasury, at Commerce,&nbsp;&nbsp; at Energy. They're all over the place. Just&nbsp; because there is intelligence that comes in every&nbsp;&nbsp; day that needs to be shared with the leadership&nbsp; of those departments, right? Maybe you pick up&nbsp;&nbsp; something about threats to air travel or about&nbsp; some germ that's been developed somewhere, or&nbsp;&nbsp; you stole the Japanese trade-negotiation talking&nbsp; points and you need to share them or whatever. But&nbsp;&nbsp; "identity intelligence"—it doesn't have a meaning&nbsp; for me. I'm not sure what that is.All I can think&nbsp;&nbsp; of is HHS has clients. Maybe they want to know if&nbsp; they're US citizens or they want to know who they&nbsp;&nbsp; are. But that would normally fall under the&nbsp; Office of Security.Yeah. All right. So yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp; I have no clue here.Oh, Rebellious—Rebellious,&nbsp; I remembered. Just to say "Rebellious" instead&nbsp;&nbsp; of the whole Rebellious Rainbow Unicorn. It&nbsp; says "identity access" is cybersecurity.That